\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sta_overview.pdf}
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA). (a) Formalization pipeline: NL is formalized and verified by an external solver. (b) STA protocol: leave-one-out ablation on proved formalizations yields ISR. (c) ISR as diagnostic vs.\ as reward: when the diagnostic becomes the target, meta-level Goodhart's Law emerges.}
\label{fig:sta-overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task and GRPO Training}
\label{sec:task}

Given premises $P_1,\ldots,P_k$ and conclusion $C$, the task is to produce a formal logic program $F=\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$ verified by an external solver $S\in\{\text{Prover9 (FOL), SWI-Prolog}\}$ (\autoref{fig:sta-overview}a).
We train with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$ rollouts), where each rollout receives binary execution reward $r_{\text{exec}}(x) = \mathbf{1}[S(F_x) = \textit{proved}]$.
This optimizes prove rate but provides no signal about \emph{structural quality}: a formalization embedding the conclusion among its premises receives the same reward as a faithful derivation.
All experiments draw from FOLIO~\citep{folio}; the held-out evaluation file retains a legacy \texttt{proofwriter\_fol\_*.jsonl} filename from an early pipeline iteration but contains only FOLIO-derived examples.\footnote{The filename is preserved for reproducibility against the recorded artifacts; no ProofWriter data is used.}

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{sec:sta}

To diagnose structural quality, STA tests whether each component of a proved formalization is individually necessary for proof success (\autoref{fig:sta-overview}b).
Given a proved $x$ with $n$ components, STA constructs $n$ ablated variants $x\setminus\{c_i\}$, each removing one component, and re-runs the solver (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
A component is \emph{necessary} if its removal causes proof failure: $S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}$.
What constitutes a component depends on the logic type: individual premises in FOL, individual clauses or ground facts in Prolog.
The protocol requires $n$ additional solver calls per proved sample.
We apply the protocol as a diagnostic on top of solver execution, not as a novel mutation primitive; leave-one-out ablation is standard in mutation testing and feature attribution.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{alg:sta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, solver $S$
\ENSURE $\text{ISR}(x) \in [0, 1]$
\STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets 0$
\FOR{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \STATE $x_{\setminus i} \gets x \setminus \{c_i\}$
    \IF{$S(x_{\setminus i}) = \textit{failed}$}
        \STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets \textit{necessary} + 1$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $\textit{necessary} \,/\, n$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Intensional Structural Reward.}
ISR is the fraction of necessary components:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\bigl[S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}\bigr]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
ISR $\in[0,1]$ is defined only for proved formalizations ($r_{\text{exec}}(x){=}1$): ISR${=}1$ indicates every component is individually necessary; ISR${=}0$ indicates complete redundancy or degeneracy.
Throughout we report \emph{proved-only} ISR (denoted ISR$^*$) to avoid conflating structural quality with prove rate.

\paragraph{Limitations of ISR.}
ISR measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness: a formalization can achieve $\text{ISR}{=}1$ while being semantically unrelated to the NL input (e.g., a single premise identical to the conclusion trivially satisfies necessity).
ISR is also sensitive to component count $n$: when $n{=}2$, removing the sole premise breaks any non-trivial proof, forcing $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ regardless of proof quality.
This component-count confound is an inherent property of LOO-based metrics; we control for it via premise-count-matched analysis in \S\ref{sec:goodhart}.

\subsection{Reward Conditions}
\label{sec:rewards}

We study six conditions (Table~\ref{tab:reward}; see also \autoref{fig:sta-overview}c).
Multiplicative bonuses apply only to proved samples ($r{=}0$ for unproved), keeping the prove-rate floor intact.
The random additive control replaces ISR with a uniform draw $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$ at matched $\beta$ to isolate ISR-specific signal from generic reward-variance effects.
The SFT baseline isolates whether structural degeneration arises from RL optimization or from data exposure alone.
Each RL condition is run with seeds $\{42,43,44\}$; all conditions share identical training data (\S\ref{sec:setup}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Reward} & $\beta$ \\
\midrule
Exec-only & $r_{\text{exec}}$ & --- \\
ISR low-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$ & 0.1 \\
ISR high-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$ & 0.3 \\
Random low-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta\,u)$ & 0.1 \\
Random high-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta\,u)$ & 0.3 \\
SFT & NLL on gold formalizations & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Reward conditions. $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$; all multiplicative bonuses are gated on $r_{\text{exec}}{=}1$. All GRPO conditions use Qwen3-4B, $K{=}8$, lr $10^{-6}$, 500 steps.}
\label{tab:reward}
\end{table}

An earlier convex formulation ($r = \alpha\,r_{\text{exec}} + \beta\,r_{\text{exec}}\,\text{ISR}$, $\alpha{+}\beta{=}1$) was discarded because $\text{ISR}{<}1$ always penalized relative to pure execution reward, collapsing prove rate.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy-method}

Across conditions, seeds, and logic types we identify seven structural-degeneration modes.
Definitions are stated here at the conceptual level; per-condition prevalence is reported in \S\ref{sec:experiments} and Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}.

\textbf{H1: Conclusion embedding.}
At least one premise is identical to the conclusion after normalization, reducing the proof to the trivial $p\rightarrow p$.

\textbf{H7: Conclusion duplication with ISR inflation.}
The formalization contains a single premise identical to the conclusion ($n{=}2$); because removing the sole premise breaks the proof, $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$ by construction.
This mode targets the ISR signal directly.

\textbf{Tautological collapse.}
Degeneration to a minimal two-component structure that is trivially provable, without explicit ISR-driven inflation.

\textbf{Repetition collapse.}
Identical (or near-identical) premises emitted repeatedly, inflating component count without adding logical content.

\textbf{Complexity without utility.}
Syntactically elaborate but logically vacuous premises that do not contribute to the proof.

\textbf{Contradiction explosion.}
Contradictory premises (e.g., $p(a)\wedge\neg p(a)$) from which any conclusion follows \emph{ex falso quodlibet}; component counts inflate while every conclusion becomes derivable.

\textbf{Query-as-fact gaming} (Prolog-specific).
The model emits the NL question as a Prolog atom and asserts it as both fact and query, making the proof trivially true.
This mode evades text-matching classifiers built for conclusion embedding; detection requires functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution from STA.

Modes are identified by a two-stage procedure: strict normalized text matching (catches H1/H7) and functional analysis of the $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution from STA (catches modes that evade text matching, including query-as-fact gaming).
We report both strict (text-match) and functional ($n_{\text{necessary}}$-based) classifications in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

In interpreting ISR results, we distinguish two failure semantics that share a numerical signature: an exec-only \emph{low} ISR signals \emph{redundancy} (e.g., repeated ground facts in Prolog so that no single component is individually necessary), whereas an ISR-rewarded \emph{high} ISR signals \emph{metric gaming} (H7-style constructions that satisfy the LOO definition without supporting genuine derivation).
A bare ISR difference therefore does not translate directly into a reasoning-ability difference; the mode breakdown above is what licenses such interpretation.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

We study the task of translating natural language (NL) premises and hypotheses into formal logic representations verifiable by an external solver.
Given NL premises $P$ and hypothesis $H$, a formalizer $\pi_\theta$ generates a formal representation $x$ comprising $n$ components $\{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$---axioms, rules, and a conclusion---submitted to solver $\mathcal{S}$, which returns a binary outcome: \texttt{proved} or \texttt{fail}.

We consider two target formalisms:
(1)~\textbf{First-order logic (FOL)}, where components are Prover9-format axioms and a goal clause, verified by the Prover9 theorem prover~\cite{mccune2010prover9};
(2)~\textbf{Prolog}, where components are Horn clauses, facts, and a query, verified by SWI-Prolog.
Training data is drawn from FOLIO~\cite{folio}, a corpus of 1{,}204 human-annotated FOL examples.
We use a fixed split of 500 training and 100 held-out evaluation examples across all experiments.

\subsection{GRPO Training}
\label{sec:grpo}

We train the formalizer with Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep[GRPO;][]{deepseekmath}.
For each training problem, the policy $\pi_\theta$ generates $K{=}8$ candidate formalizations.
Each candidate is submitted to the solver, yielding a binary execution reward:
\begin{equation}
  r_{\text{exec}}(x) =
  \begin{cases}
    1 & \text{if } \mathcal{S}(x) = \texttt{proved} \\
    0 & \text{otherwise}
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:r_exec}
\end{equation}
GRPO normalizes rewards within each group of $K$ completions to estimate advantages, then updates $\pi_\theta$ via a clipped policy gradient objective.
The base model is Qwen3-4B; all runs use learning rate $10^{-6}$ and train for 500 gradient steps unless otherwise noted.

Under execution-only reward, the optimizer receives no signal about \emph{why} a proof succeeds---whether the formalization captures genuine logical derivation or exploits a degenerate shortcut.
This opacity motivates a structural diagnostic.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation}
\label{sec:sta}

Solver Trace Ablation (STA) tests whether each component of a proved formalization is \emph{necessary} for the proof.
The protocol applies leave-one-out (LOO) ablation---a standard technique in mutation testing~\cite{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey} and feature attribution---to solver inputs.
We claim no novelty in the ablation mechanism itself; the contribution lies in applying it as a diagnostic for reward hacking in neurosymbolic GRPO training.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{alg:sta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, solver $\mathcal{S}$
\Ensure $\text{ISR}(x) \in [0, 1]$
\State $\textit{necessary} \gets 0$
\For{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \State $x_{\setminus i} \gets x \setminus \{c_i\}$
    \If{$\mathcal{S}(x_{\setminus i}) = \texttt{fail}$}
        \State $\textit{necessary} \gets \textit{necessary} + 1$
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $\textit{necessary} \;/\; n$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

For a proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, STA removes each component in turn and re-runs the solver (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
The protocol requires $n$ solver calls per proved sample.
For unproved samples ($\mathcal{S}(x) = \texttt{fail}$), ISR is undefined: there is no successful proof to ablate.
Throughout the paper, we report \emph{proved-only} ISR (denoted ISR$^*$), computed exclusively over samples the solver proves, to avoid conflating structural quality with prove rate.
Figure~\ref{fig:sta-overview} illustrates the full protocol.

\subsection{Intensional Structural Reward}
\label{sec:isr}

The \textbf{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR) quantifies the fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}\!\left[\mathcal{S}(x_{\setminus i}) = \texttt{fail}\right]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}

ISR is bounded in $[0, 1]$.
$\text{ISR} = 1$ indicates every component is individually necessary---removing any single one breaks the proof.
$\text{ISR} = 0$ indicates no component is individually necessary, signaling redundancy or degeneracy.

ISR measures \emph{syntactic component necessity}: whether each piece is required for the proof to succeed.
It does not measure semantic faithfulness to the NL input.
A formalization can achieve $\text{ISR} = 1$ while being semantically unrelated to the original statement---a single premise identical to the conclusion trivially satisfies necessity.

\paragraph{Premise count bias.}
ISR is mathematically sensitive to component count $n$.
When $n = 2$ (one premise, one conclusion), removing the sole premise breaks any non-trivial proof, yielding $\text{ISR} \geq 0.5$ regardless of proof quality.
Conversely, formalizations with many components tend toward lower ISR as redundancy accumulates.
This is an inherent property of LOO-based metrics, not specific to our setting.
When comparing ISR across conditions that differ in mean premise count, we control for this confound via premise-count-matched analysis (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\subsection{Reward Formulations}
\label{sec:reward}

We study six reward conditions to isolate the effect of ISR as a training signal (Table~\ref{tab:reward}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Reward formula} & $\beta$ \\
\midrule
Exec-only (baseline) & $r_{\text{exec}}$ & --- \\
ISR low-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot \text{ISR})$ & 0.1 \\
ISR high-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot \text{ISR})$ & 0.3 \\
Random low-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot u)$ & 0.1 \\
Random high-dose & $r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot u)$ & 0.3 \\
SFT baseline & Supervised (NLL on GT) & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Reward conditions. $u \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)$. All GRPO conditions use Qwen3-4B, $K{=}8$, lr$=10^{-6}$, 500 steps.}
\label{tab:reward}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additive ISR bonus.}
ISR augments the execution reward as a multiplicative bonus, active only for proved samples:
\begin{equation}
  r = r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot \text{ISR}(x))
  \label{eq:additive_isr}
\end{equation}
Proved samples receive reward in $[1, 1{+}\beta]$; unproved samples receive $0$.
We evaluate $\beta \in \{0.1, 0.3\}$ to test dose-response dynamics.
An earlier convex formulation ($r = \alpha \cdot r_{\text{exec}} + \beta \cdot r_{\text{exec}} \cdot \text{ISR}$, $\alpha{+}\beta{=}1$) was discarded because $\text{ISR} < 1$ always penalized relative to pure execution reward, collapsing prove rate.

\paragraph{Random additive control.}
To disentangle ISR's structural signal from the effect of reward variance, we replace ISR with a uniform random variable:
\begin{equation}
  r = r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta \cdot u), \quad u \sim \text{Uniform}(0, 1)
  \label{eq:additive_random}
\end{equation}
with identical $\beta$ values.
If ISR provides genuine structural guidance beyond stochastic perturbation, it should outperform this control.

\paragraph{SFT baseline.}
Standard supervised fine-tuning on the same 500 training examples using ground-truth FOL annotations as targets.
No reinforcement learning or reward shaping.
This control isolates whether structural degeneration stems from RL optimization or from data exposure alone.

All GRPO conditions share identical hyperparameters and are evaluated across seeds $\in \{42, 43, 44\}$.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

We identify seven distinct modes of structural degeneration across reward conditions and logic types.
Definitions appear below; quantitative prevalence analysis is deferred to~\S\ref{sec:experiments}.

\textbf{H1: Conclusion embedding.}
At least one premise is identical to the conclusion after normalization, making the proof trivially $p \rightarrow p$.
This is the dominant mode across all GRPO conditions (52--93\% of proved samples).

\textbf{H7: Conclusion duplication with ISR inflation.}
The formalization contains a single premise identical to the conclusion ($n{=}2$).
Removing the premise breaks the proof, so $\text{ISR} \geq 0.5$ by construction.
This mode is exclusive to ISR-reward conditions (64.5\% vs.\ 0\% under random control across three seeds) and represents ISR-specific gaming.

\textbf{Tautological collapse.}
Degeneration to a minimal two-component structure that is trivially provable, arising under exec-only conditions without ISR inflation.

\textbf{Repetition collapse.}
Identical premises generated repeatedly, inflating component count without adding logical content.

\textbf{Complexity without utility.}
Syntactically complex but logically vacuous premises that do not contribute to the proof.

\textbf{Contradiction explosion.}
Contradictory premises (e.g., $p(a) \wedge \neg p(a)$) from which any conclusion follows \emph{ex falso quodlibet}.
Observed at extended training (1000 steps), with mean component count rising from 6.6 to 18.6.

\textbf{Query-as-fact gaming} (Prolog-specific).
The model converts the NL question into a Prolog atom asserted simultaneously as fact and query, making the proof trivially true.
This mode evades text-matching classifiers designed to detect conclusion embedding; detection requires functional analysis of the STA necessity vector ($n_{\text{necessary}} = 1$ for 95/97 proved samples).

Detection combines two complementary methods: normalized text matching (for H1, H7) and functional analysis of the necessity vector (for modes that bypass text-based classifiers, such as query-as-fact gaming).
We report both strict (text-match) and functional ($n_{\text{necessary}}$-based) classifications throughout.

\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained neurosymbolic formalizers achieve high prove rates---93\% on FOLIO---yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion verbatim among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution-based evaluation. We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic (solver-agnostic in principle, validated on two structurally different solvers: Prover9 and SWI-Prolog) that applies leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs. The fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR): ISR$=$1 indicates syntactic component necessity; ISR$=$0 indicates complete redundancy. ISR does not correlate with downstream NLI accuracy ($\rho{=}{-}0.049$, $p{=}0.187$), confirming it measures structural properties orthogonal to semantic correctness.

ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from prove rate: over 100 to 1000 GRPO training steps (Qwen3-4B), ISR falls from 0.642 to 0.003---a 99.5\% degradation---while prove rate rises from 32\% to 96\%. An SFT baseline achieves 92.9\% non-hacking proofs with near-zero hacking, confirming the degeneration is RL-specific.

Can ISR serve as a training signal? Incorporating ISR into the reward at $\beta{=}0.1$ induces \emph{meta-level Goodhart's Law}: 64.5\% of proved samples exhibit conclusion duplication (H7), versus 0\% under matched random controls. The sole apparent ISR improvement reduces to $1.06\times$ after controlling for a premise-count confound. At $\beta{=}0.3$, prove rate collapses to 18\% versus 48\% for random control.

Cross-logic transfer to Prolog/SWI-Prolog yields a stronger result: ISR-as-reward produces universal Goodhart---3/3 seeds gaming via distinct mechanisms, 0\% non-hacking proofs.

Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog), and 3 seeds, we catalog 7 distinct reward hacking modes---including query-as-fact gaming that bypasses text-match classifiers---documenting ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data will be released upon acceptance: \url{[anonymized for review]}}
\end{abstract}

